          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-48cb2a607749\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"“Je t’aime toujours” directly avows the speaker’s continuing love despite the addressee’s prior conduct. It primarily presents an emotional state as true. The accompanying wish to keep the addressee present explains the avowal’s relational stakes but does not make the love clause an undertaking, and there is no positive evidence of reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The surrounding wish to retain the addressee could give the exchange a weak commitment-like implication, but the text explicitly frames it as desire (“je veux”), not as a promise or undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A character directly tells the addressee, in dialogue, “je t’aime toujours” (“I still love you”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The speaker offers continuing love as the reason for wanting the addressee to remain: “despite what you have done to me” marks injury or grievance, while “still” emphasizes that the loving state persists. This concessive framing strengthens the force of the avowal without independently establishing performative commitment or expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« malgré ce que tu m'as fait, je t'aime toujours »\",\"supports\":\"The concessive clause and “toujours” present love as a persisting emotional state despite prior harm, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Parce que … je t'aime toujours et que je veux te garder ici. »\",\"supports\":\"“Parce que” makes the love avowal part of an explanation, while the coordinated desire to keep the addressee present supplies relational context but no explicit promise or undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is located in Chapter XXIV of Alexandre Dumas fils’s La Dame aux camélias, in French direct-dialogue formatting.\",\"supports\":\"Supports identifying the event as a direct fictional utterance rather than narration, reported speech, or writing.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal of continuing love. The grievance and wish to retain the addressee are contextual qualifications adequately represented outside the core labels; no residual force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification relies on the supplied excerpt and metadata; no wider-work plot knowledge was needed.\"}"
        }
